---
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Titan Axial Compressor Design and CFD Study"
  category="Turbomachinery · CFD · Aerodynamic Design"
  status="Completed"
  summary="Design and transient CFD validation of a single-stage axial compressor for Titan atmospheric conditions."
  tools={["ANSYS BladeGen", "ANSYS TurboGrid", "ANSYS CFX", "CFX-Post"]}
  team="Three-Person / Individual Course Project · ME 3470 Aeronautical Propulsion"
>
  <div class="case-study-page">
    <section class="case-study-card context-card">
      <div class="context-icon-slot">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/titan-icon.png"
          alt="Titan icon"
        />
      </div>

      <div class="context-copy">
        <h2>Titan Axial Compressor Stage</h2>
        <p>
          Here, I worked partially with a team and partially by myself
          to design a stage of an axial compressor for a turbojet engine from 
          scratch in ANSYS CFX. The atmospheric conditions and fluid moving through 
          the compressor stage were based on Titan atmospheric conditions 
          at 8 km altitude. This process involved a great deal of 
          research, design, self-teaching, trial and error, and time 
          to get the results that we were looking for. The idea of the project is 
          to be the first step at designing a turbojet engine for functionality 
          on Titan since it is the next candidate for a habitable planet for humans, after Mars. 
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="context-icon-slot">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/titan-icon.png"
          alt="Titan icon"
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study-card split-card reverse-card">
      <div class="titan-atmosphere-visual">
        <div class="titan-atmosphere-frame">
          <img
            src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/titan-atmosphere-profile.jpg"
            alt="Elevation versus temperature graph of Titan's atmosphere"
          />
        </div>

        <div class="titan-atmosphere-caption">
          Elevation vs. Temperature Graph of Titan's Atmosphere
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <h2>Titan Atmospheric Conditions</h2>
        <p>
          Before beginning the compressor design, the team had to establish 
          an optimal altitude to operate at on Titan, the atmospheric 
          conditions at that altitude, and how those items shaped 
          the inlet conditions of the compressor. The data researched and calculated 
          by the team can be seen below.
        </p>

<table class="data-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th colspan="2">Titan Atmospheric Data at 8 km</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>

  <tbody>

    <tr>
      <td>Gas Composition</td>
      <td>95% Nitrogen, 5% Methane</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Static Pressure</td>
      <td>97.5 kPa</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Total Pressure</td>
      <td>117.6 kPa</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Static Temperature</td>
      <td>84.5 K</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Total Temperature</td>
      <td>89.1 K</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Density</td>
      <td>3.70 kg/m³</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
      <td>Mixture Gas Constant</td>
      <td>307.9 J/(kg·K)</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study-card split-card">
      <div class="section-copy">
        <h2>Preliminary Aerodynamic Design</h2>
        <p>
          The compressor was designed as a single-stage IGV–Rotor–Stator system with a target total pressure ratio of
          1.10, an axial velocity near 100 m/s, and a preliminary isentropic compressor efficiency target of 80%. The geometry of the 
          compressor stage was a relatively small scale, having the peak shroud radius being 80 mm. Given the atmospheric conditions
           and small geometry of the stage, the rotor speed was set to 17,300 rpm. This speed 
           ensured that Mach numbers throughout the stage would remain below 1, allowing no shocks 
           to occur.
        </p>
        <p>
          Utilizing the preliminary inlet velocity, outlet velocity, compressor ratio, 
          rotor rotational speed, inlet conditions, and isentropic efficiency, 
          I determined the amount of total temperature and in turn circumferential velocity that needed to be applied to the flow 
          in order to achieve the desired pressure ratio. With these items, I was able to start designing the blades.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="carousel captioned-carousel" data-carousel>
        <div class="carousel-frame">
          <div class="carousel-slide is-active" data-slide>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/compressor-design-inputs.png"
              alt="Preliminary compressor design inputs"
            />
            <div class="carousel-caption">Preliminary Design Inputs</div>
          </div>

          <div class="carousel-slide" data-slide>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/compressor-work-to-swirl.png"
              alt="Work requirement to rotor-exit swirl calculation"
            />
            <div class="carousel-caption">Work Requirement to Rotor-Exit Swirl</div>
          </div>

          <button class="carousel-arrow previous" type="button" data-previous aria-label="Previous preliminary aerodynamic design image">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 5l-7 7 7 7" /></svg>
          </button>
          <button class="carousel-arrow next" type="button" data-next aria-label="Next preliminary aerodynamic design image">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" /></svg>
          </button>
        </div>

        <div class="carousel-dots" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="is-active" data-dot></span>
          <span data-dot></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study-card split-card reverse-card">
      <div class="carousel captioned-carousel" data-carousel>
        <div class="carousel-frame">
          <div class="carousel-slide is-active" data-slide>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/bladegen3IGV.png"
              alt="ANSYS BladeGen IGV blade geometry"
            />
            <div class="carousel-caption">IGV Blade Geometry</div>
          </div>

          <div class="carousel-slide" data-slide>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/bladegen3drotor.png"
              alt="ANSYS BladeGen rotor blade geometry"
            />
            <div class="carousel-caption">Rotor Blade Geometry</div>
          </div>

          <div class="carousel-slide" data-slide>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/bladegen3dstator.png"
              alt="ANSYS BladeGen stator blade geometry"
            />
            <div class="carousel-caption">Stator Blade Geometry</div>
          </div>

          <button class="carousel-arrow previous" type="button" data-previous aria-label="Previous blade design image">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 5l-7 7 7 7" /></svg>
          </button>
          <button class="carousel-arrow next" type="button" data-next aria-label="Next blade design image">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" /></svg>
          </button>
        </div>

        <div class="carousel-dots" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="is-active" data-dot></span>
          <span data-dot></span>
          <span data-dot></span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <h2>Blade Design & Velocity Triangles</h2>
        <p>
          The aerodynamic requirements were converted into blade geometry through various equations. This 
          geometry was input in ANSYS BladeGen to design the blades. Spanwise velocity-triangle
          calculations based on axial flow velocity and necessary tangential flow velocity were used to choose the inlet and 
          outlet metal angles for the blade rows, with the free-vortex distribution carried through the rotor design.
          The final stage used 18 IGVs, 18 rotor blades, and 24 stator vanes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study-card split-card">
      <div class="section-copy">
        <h2>Meshing & CFD Model Setup</h2>
        <p>
          The blade rows were meshed in ANSYS TurboGrid, capped at 1 million nodes due to the student license being used, 
          but had minimal skewness. The meshed blades were solved in ANSYS CFX using the SST turbulence model and Total
          Energy formulation. The IGV and stator remained stationary while the rotor operated at 17,300 rpm. Steady
          Frozen Rotor simulations were used for design screening before the final design was evaluated with a Transient
          Rotor–Stator model that would start from the Frozen Rotor convergence.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some other facts about the simulation and design was that a 60° periodic sector represented 3 IGV passages, 3 rotor passages, and 4 stator passages. Rotor tip clearance
          was explicitly modeled at approximately 0.5% span. The transient timestep was
          7.2254 × 10⁻⁶ s, with 80 timesteps representing one repeating 60° blade-row interaction period.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="carousel captioned-carousel" data-carousel>
        <div class="carousel-frame">
          <div class="carousel-slide is-active" data-slide>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/turbogridIGV.png"
              alt="ANSYS TurboGrid IGV mesh"
            />
            <div class="carousel-caption">IGV Mesh</div>
          </div>

          <div class="carousel-slide" data-slide>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/turbogridrotor.png"
              alt="ANSYS TurboGrid rotor mesh"
            />
            <div class="carousel-caption">Rotor Mesh</div>
          </div>

          <div class="carousel-slide" data-slide>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/turbogridstator.png"
              alt="ANSYS TurboGrid stator mesh"
            />
            <div class="carousel-caption">Stator Mesh</div>
          </div>

          <button class="carousel-arrow previous" type="button" data-previous aria-label="Previous meshing and CFD setup image">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 5l-7 7 7 7" /></svg>
          </button>
          <button class="carousel-arrow next" type="button" data-next aria-label="Next meshing and CFD setup image">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" /></svg>
          </button>
        </div>

        <div class="carousel-dots" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="is-active" data-dot></span>
          <span data-dot></span>
          <span data-dot></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study-card split-card reverse-card">
      <div class="carousel captioned-carousel" data-carousel>
        <div class="carousel-frame">
          <div class="carousel-slide is-active" data-slide>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/full-stage-assembly.png"
              alt="Full compressor stage blade-row assembly"
            />
            <div class="carousel-caption">Full Stage Blade-Row Assembly</div>
          </div>

          <div class="carousel-slide" data-slide>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/full-stage-velocity-vectors.png"
              alt="Full compressor stage with velocity vectors"
            />
            <div class="carousel-caption">Full Stage with Velocity Vectors</div>
          </div>

          <div class="carousel-slide" data-slide>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/titan-axial-compressor/latesttransientrun2.webp"
              alt="Final transient rotor-stator simulation"
            />
            <div class="carousel-caption">Final Transient Simulation</div>
          </div>

          <button class="carousel-arrow previous" type="button" data-previous aria-label="Previous design iteration and validation image">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 5l-7 7 7 7" /></svg>
          </button>
          <button class="carousel-arrow next" type="button" data-next aria-label="Next design iteration and validation image">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" /></svg>
          </button>
        </div>

        <div class="carousel-dots" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="is-active" data-dot></span>
          <span data-dot></span>
          <span data-dot></span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-copy">
        <h2>Design Iteration & Final Validation</h2>
        <p>
          In the original design, the stage was simulated using a constant-area annulus which allowed axial velocity to fall as density increased through the stage.
          To fix this, I slowly increased the contraction from the rotor inlet to the stator outlet, 
          moving more of the contraction to the stator region than rotor due to density primarily increasing in the stator region. Figuring 
          out the area change necessary involved calculations and trial-and-error to avoid creating mach speeds at the outlet.
        </p>
        <p>
          After some tweaks for a smooth area transition, I was able to roughly maintain the 100 m/s outlet axial 
          velocity design target. I finished the model obtaining a 1.116 pressure ratio, 82.4% isentropic 
          compressor efficiency, and 100.093 m/s outlet velocity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="overall-section">
      <div class="overall-heading">
        <span></span>
        <h2>Overall</h2>
        <span></span>
      </div>

      <div class="overall-grid">
        <article class="overall-card">
          <h3>Results</h3>
          <p>
            The final transient solution reached a total pressure ratio of 1.116 and a settled 
            isentropic compressor efficiency of approximately 82.4%. The outlet axial velocity was 
            100.093 m/s with only 1.864 m/s of circumferential velocity. This produced an outlet flow angle of 88.94°, 
            nearly pure axial flow.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="overall-card">
          <h3>My Contributions</h3>
          <p>
            While the preliminary research for Titan atmospheric conditions 
            was done with my group, all of the work related to designing the compressor blades, 
            running calculations, interpreting results, and redoing if insufficient were done by me. 
            I spent roughly two months on this project doing a lot of reserach on my own and learning how 
            to operate BladeGen from scratch. 
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="overall-card">
          <h3>Lessons Learned</h3>
          <p>
            I learned a lot about blade design and how axial compressors operate. Further, I 
            became very familiar with CFX and BladeGen, improving my ability to diagnose problems and 
            test out solutions. I also learned about common forms of loss that are found with blades such as 
            tip vortexes, friction, blade wakes, adverse-pressure gradients, amongst several other factors that 
            can be targeted in the future to improve the efficiency of this design.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</ProjectLayout>

<style>
